ine the light of Your face upon us, O LORD.","verse":6},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"I will lie down and sleep in peace, for You alone, O LORD, make me dwell in safety.","verse":8}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.3.1-7.17","r":"Ps.3.1-7.17","rawR":"Ps.3.1-7.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.3.1-7.17","value":"Ps.3.1-7.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 3–7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This group of psalms moves readers from the orderly world of ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.1.1-2.12","r":"Ps.1.1-2.12","rawR":"Ps.1.1-2.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.1.1-2.12","value":"Ps.1.1-2.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 1–2"}," to a disoriented one. The Lord’s anointed cries out while facing enemies, ferocious opposition, evil schemes, and the Lord’s apparent distance."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.3.1-7.17","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":7,"endVerse":17,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.3.1-7.17","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[3,4,5,6,7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"979e35739df35cd8a906cb35ce271f91dc796183d42ddf70cbc0fa127889810f","sourceItemName":"Ps.3.1-7.